## Summary
This adds game components. These will be used to write games and get attached to entities. It's a Unity-style approach.

You simply define a class, inherit from tmt::GameComponent, and register it:
```cpp
class MoveUp : public tmt::GameComponent<MoveUp> {
   public:
    using GameComponent::GameComponent;

    static constexpr std::string_view name() { return "Move Up"; }

    void start() override {};
    void update(const tmt::FrameData& time) override;
    void end() override {};

    float speed = 1.0f;
};
TMT_OBJECT(MoveUp, (speed));
```
```cpp
/* Register Components */
tmt::engine.component_registry.register_component<MoveUp>();
tmt::engine.component_registry.register_component<MoveSide>();
tmt::engine.component_registry.register_component<MoveForward>();
```
You can now either add the component through code:
```cpp
tmt::Entity entity = tmt::engine.ecs.create_entity("Camera");
tmt::engine.ecs.add_component<MoveUp>(entity);
```
Or through the editor:
<img width="553" height="528" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/ac5353d5-6980-4b14-9714-d480c13608d6" />

The ``start``, ``update``, and ``end`` function will be called automatically by the engine.

Additionally, inside the component, you have access to ``entity`` which is the entity the component is attached to.

### Why?
This simplifies/quickens making game aspects and gives the engine an interface for updating the game, inspecting, and serialization.

## Testing
Try making your own component.
1. Make a class
2. Inherit from ``tmt::GameComponent`` and give your class as template
3. Add these lines to your class (required):
```cpp
using GameComponent::GameComponent;
static constexpr std::string_view name() { return "Your Component"; }
```
4. override ``start``, ``update``, and ``end``
5. Do something in the update function. (note that you have access to ``entity``)
6. Register your function in ``create_application`` by calling:
```cpp
tmt::engine.component_registry.register_component<MoveUp>();
```
7. Add the component either through code or in the editor
8. Run the game, and your component should be getting called.

